patches.behavior:
State per loop index: `{ lastPosition: [0, 0], lastLocal: [0, 0], blocked: false }`.

Each frame:
```
const snap = ctx.services.pointer(ctx.input("layer")); // null = whole screen
const enabled = ctx.input("enabled");
if (!enabled && (snap.down || snap.ended)) state.blocked = true;
const live = enabled && !state.blocked;
if (live && (snap.down || snap.ended)) {
  state.lastPosition = snap.position;
  state.lastLocal = snap.localPosition;
}
ctx.output("down", live && snap.down);
if (live && snap.tapped) ctx.pulse("tap");
ctx.output("position", state.lastPosition);
ctx.output("localPosition", state.lastLocal);
ctx.output("force", live && snap.down ? snap.pressure : 0);
if (!snap.down) state.blocked = false; // a blocked press is over once every pointer lifts
```

- Pulses: `tap` is the only pulse and lasts one frame. Two fingers lifting on the same frame give one pulse.
- Tap rule (ARCHITECTURE.md §5.5, CONVENTIONS.md §18.5.1): the press began on the layer or a descendant, never moved 10 pt or more from its start, wasn't cancelled, and lifted over the layer (the release hit chain contains it). There's no time limit, so a long hold still taps.
- Down is captured: true from the press frame while any pointer that began on the layer stays down, even after sliding off; false on the release frame, the same frame Tap fires.
- Position: prototype coordinates (points from the root's top-left, y down). While pressed it follows the first pointer still down; on the release frame it's the release point; afterward it holds. [0, 0] before the first press. Mouse movement without a press doesn't change it (that's Hover).
- Local Position: the same pointer mapped through the inverse of the layer's world transform (layer-local points from its top-left). With no layer it equals Position.
- Force: pointer pressure 0–1 while down, otherwise 0. Mouse presses report 0; touch screens without pressure sensing report 0.5 while pressed (web platform behavior).
- Hit testing runs front to back on the previous frame's layout (ARCHITECTURE.md §5.1). Layers that are disabled, at opacity 0, or with Receives Touches off never receive presses, not even through their children.
- Frame 0: idle outputs; pointer events dispatched before frame 0 apply on frame 0.
- Loops: when Layer refers to a layer replicated by a loop, `ctx.input("layer")` carries the copy's `instance`. The patch evaluates once per copy, every output is a loop, and state is per index. New indices start idle; removed indices drop their state.
- Edge cases: a missing layer id, or a layer that isn't rendered this frame, reads as never pressed.
- Disabled: Down false, no Tap, Force 0, positions hold. A press that was held during any disabled frame produces neither Down nor Tap until it lifts. Enabled doesn't change hit testing, so touches don't pass through to layers behind.
- Muted: idle outputs and no pulses; Enabled is not passed through to Down.
- Platform and simulation: identical in the desktop viewer, the web player, and headless simulation, which reads `pointer` InputEvents. Force needs a `pressure` field on `PointerSnapshot` (contract request); until the contract has it, Force outputs 0.
- Restart and dispose: state resets and the engine's pointer tracker forgets held presses, so a finger held across a restart must lift and press again. Nothing to dispose.
- Coming from Origami: Origami's Position is relative to the parent's center and resets to 0 on touch-up, so tap-position recipes needed Delay 1; Sonobe keeps the release point on the Tap frame (gap-fill R5). Force maps 0–6.67 to 0–1. The Quartz-era Interaction re-tested Down every frame; Sonobe captures the press.

State per loop index: `{ lastPosition: [0, 0], lastLocal: [0, 0], lastStart: [0, 0], blocked: false }`.

Each frame:
```
const snap = ctx.services.pointer(ctx.input("layer")); // null = whole screen
const enabled = ctx.input("enabled");
if (!enabled && (snap.down || snap.ended)) state.blocked = true;
const live = enabled && !state.blocked;
const active = live && (snap.down || snap.ended); // pressed now, or released this frame
if (active) {
  state.lastPosition = snap.position;
  state.lastLocal = snap.localPosition;
  state.lastStart = snap.startPosition;
}
ctx.output("down", live && snap.down);
if (live && snap.tapped) ctx.pulse("tap");
ctx.output("position", state.lastPosition);
ctx.output("translation", active ? snap.translation : [0, 0]);
ctx.output("velocity", active ? snap.velocity : [0, 0]);
ctx.output("startPosition", state.lastStart);
ctx.output("localPosition", state.lastLocal);
if (!snap.down) state.blocked = false;
```

- Pulses: `tap` follows Interaction's tap rule (began on the layer, moved less than 10 pt, not cancelled, lifted over the layer).
- Translation: `position − startPosition` of the tracked pointer, in prototype axes (not rotated into the layer). [0, 0] on the press frame. No dead zone: movement inside the 10 pt tap slop still shows.
- Velocity: the engine's per-pointer estimate (ARCHITECTURE.md §5.5, gap-fill R9), in points per second on each axis. It's an exponential moving average of displacement over time with a 25 ms time constant; the press frame adds no sample, so there's no first-frame spike; after about 50 ms without movement it decays toward 0; a release frame without new movement keeps the last estimate. The estimate is time-based, so it matches at 60 and 120 fps.
- Release frame: Down false, Tap may fire, and Translation and Velocity keep their final values. The next frame both read [0, 0]. Spring Animation samples Gesture Velocity on the frame Gesture Active falls, which is this frame. Decision: returning to [0, 0] lets a spring driven by Translation fly home by default.
- Position and Local Position behave as on Interaction: live while pressed, the release point on the release frame, held afterward. Start Position holds where the last press began.
- Multi-touch: follows the first pointer still down. If that pointer lifts while another stays down, the tracked pointer switches and Translation jumps to the new pointer's offset.
- Frame 0: idle outputs.
- Loops: per copy of a loop-replicated layer, with per-index state, as on Interaction.
- Edge cases: a missing layer reads as never pressed. Velocity never outputs NaN; the engine clamps sample intervals to 1/480–0.1 s.
- Disabled: idle outputs (Down false, Translation and Velocity [0, 0], positions hold); a press held during any disabled frame stays ignored until it lifts.
- Muted: idle outputs and no pulses; Enabled is not passed through to Down.
- Platform and simulation: same everywhere; simulation reads `pointer` InputEvents.
- Restart and dispose: state resets; nothing to dispose.
- Coming from Origami: Origami documents Velocity in points per second without saying how it's smoothed; Sonobe fixes a 25 ms average. Position there is relative to the parent's center.

State per loop index: `{ blocked: false }`.

Each frame:
```
const snap = ctx.services.pointer(ctx.input("layer")); // null = whole screen
const enabled = ctx.input("enabled");
if (!enabled && (snap.down || snap.ended)) state.blocked = true;
if (enabled && !state.blocked && snap.ended && !snap.cancelled) judge(snap);
if (!snap.down) state.blocked = false;

function judge(snap) {
  const [tx, ty] = snap.translation; // release point − press start
  const [vx, vy] = snap.velocity;    // release-frame estimate, points per second
  const axis = ctx.input("axis");
  const horizontal = axis === "horizontal" || (axis === "any" && Math.abs(tx) >= Math.abs(ty));
  const d = horizontal ? tx : ty;
  const v = horizontal ? vx : vy;
  if (Math.abs(d) < 10) return; // taps and small wiggles never swipe
  const minVelocity = Math.max(0, ctx.input("minVelocity"));
  const minDistance = Math.max(0, ctx.input("minDistance"));
  let dir = 0;
  if (v !== 0 && Math.abs(v) >= minVelocity) dir = Math.sign(v); // a flick wins, whichever way it's thrown
  else if (Math.abs(d) >= minDistance) dir = Math.sign(d);       // a slow drag must travel far enough
  if (dir === 0) return;
  ctx.pulse("swiped");
  if (horizontal) ctx.pulse(dir < 0 ? "swipedLeft" : "swipedRight");
  else ctx.pulse(dir < 0 ? "swipedUp" : "swipedDown");
}
```

- Pulses: outputs `swiped`, `swipedLeft`, `swipedRight`, `swipedUp`, `swipedDown`, one frame each. A swipe fires exactly two on the same frame: `swiped` plus one direction. Nothing fires mid-drag; the judgment happens only on the release frame.
- Axis: with Any Direction, the axis with the larger travel wins and ties go to horizontal. Horizontal and Vertical judge only that axis. Up and down follow y-down coordinates: negative y travel is Up.
- Where a press can end: it must begin on the layer or a descendant (the hit chain captured at press), and may lift anywhere, including off the layer or off the screen.
- Exclusivity: a swipe needs at least 10 pt of travel on the judged axis at release, so a press that fires Interaction's Tap (always under 10 pt) never swipes.
- Velocity: the same engine estimate Gesture reports, 25 ms average, kept on the release frame.
- Cancelled presses (the browser took over the gesture) never swipe. This needs a `cancelled` field on `PointerSnapshot` (contract request); until then, treat every `ended` press as released.
- Frame 0: no pulses unless a release is dispatched on frame 0.
- Loops: per copy of a loop-replicated layer, with per-index state; each copy pulses independently.
- Edge cases: negative Min Distance or Min Velocity count as 0. With Min Velocity 0, every release that traveled 10 pt and was still moving counts as a flick. A missing layer never swipes.
- Disabled: no pulses; a press held during any disabled frame is ignored even if it lifts while enabled.
- Platform and simulation: same everywhere; simulation reads `pointer` InputEvents, so a scripted drag path with timing produces the same velocity.
- Restart and dispose: state resets; nothing to dispose.
- Coming from Origami: no built-in equivalent. The Quartz-era Swipe macro took a Direction and output positions; Pop Switch (Swipe X or Y) covers two-state swipes and imports as Pop Switch.

State per loop index: `{ pressing: false, recognized: false, moved: false, startTime: 0, start: [0, 0], blocked: false }`.

Each frame:
```
const enabled = ctx.input("enabled");
const duration = Math.max(0, ctx.input("duration"));
const snap = ctx.isConnected("down") ? null : ctx.services.pointer(ctx.input("layer"));
const down = snap ? snap.down : ctx.input("down");

if (!enabled || state.blocked) {
  state.blocked = down; // a press that overlaps a disabled frame is ignored until it lifts
  state.pressing = false;
  state.recognized = false;
  ctx.output("longPress", false);
  ctx.output("progress", 0);
  return;
}
// A new press: Down rose, or another finger took over the pointer snapshot.
if (down && (!state.pressing || (snap && !samePoint(snap.startPosition, state.start)))) {
  state.pressing = true;
  state.recognized = false;
  state.moved = false;
  state.startTime = ctx.time;
  state.start = snap ? snap.startPosition : [0, 0];
}
let progress = 0;
if (down) {
  if (snap && !state.recognized && Math.hypot(snap.translation[0], snap.translation[1]) >= 10) state.moved = true;
  const held = ctx.time - state.startTime;
  if (!state.recognized && !state.moved && held >= duration - 1e-9) state.recognized = true;
  progress = state.recognized ? 1 : state.moved ? 0 : Math.min(1, held / duration);
  if (!state.recognized && !state.moved) ctx.requestNextFrame();
} else if (state.pressing) { // lifted this frame
  if (!state.recognized && (snap ? snap.tapped : true)) ctx.pulse("tap");
  state.pressing = false;
  state.recognized = false;
}
ctx.output("longPress", down && state.recognized);
ctx.output("progress", progress);
```

- Modes: when `down` is connected, the patch times that boolean (Origami-compatible) and ignores `layer`; there's no movement check, and every release before recognition pulses `tap`. Otherwise it watches `layer` through the pointer snapshot (null = whole screen).
- Stationary rule (ARCHITECTURE.md §5.5, gap-fill R6, CONVENTIONS.md §18.5.5): before recognition, a press whose translation reaches 10 pt is moved for good. It never turns on, Progress drops to 0, and its release doesn't tap (the snapshot's tap rule fails too). Translation is sampled once per frame.
- After recognition, movement is ignored and Long Press stays on until release. Decision: this supports sliding to a menu item; the Quartz-era macro turned off on movement.
- Timing uses `ctx.time`, so 0.5 s turns on at the same moment at 60 and 120 fps: on the first frame where held time ≥ Duration.
- Pulses: `tap` only, one frame, on the release frame of a press that wasn't recognized. There are no pulse inputs; `down` is a state.
- Release frame: Long Press and Progress are already false and 0.
- Frame 0: a Down that's already true on frame 0 starts timing on frame 0.
- Retargeting: Duration is read every frame. Lowering it mid-press can turn Long Press on at once; raising it after recognition doesn't turn it off.
- Edge cases: negative Duration counts as 0, which turns on on the press frame with Progress 1. A missing layer reads as never pressed.
- Loops: per copy of a loop-replicated layer, or per index of a looped Down, with per-index state.
- Disabled: Long Press false, Progress 0, no Tap; a press in progress is dropped and stays ignored until it lifts.
- Muted: Long Press false, Progress 0, no pulses; Enabled is not passed through.
- Platform and simulation: same everywhere; simulation reads `pointer` InputEvents.
- Restart and dispose: state resets; nothing to dispose.
- Coming from Origami: ports Down and Delay map to `down` and `duration`, and the output keeps its name. Origami's default is 0.5 s; the Quartz-era macro used 0.2 s. Setting `layer` is new and adds the stationary rule.

State per loop index: `{ pending: false, firstTapTime: 0 }`.

Each frame:
```
const enabled = ctx.input("enabled");
const interval = Math.max(0, ctx.input("interval"));
const tapped = ctx.isConnected("tap")
  ? ctx.pulsed("tap")
  : ctx.services.pointer(ctx.input("layer")).tapped; // null = whole screen
if (!enabled) {
  state.pending = false; // a waiting tap is discarded
  return;
}
const t = ctx.time;
if (tapped && state.pending && t - state.firstTapTime <= interval + 1e-9) {
  state.pending = false;
  ctx.pulse("doubleTap");
} else {
  if (state.pending && t - state.firstTapTime >= interval - 1e-9) {
    state.pending = false;
    ctx.pulse("singleTap"); // the lone tap's wait is over
  }
  if (tapped) {
    if (interval === 0) ctx.pulse("singleTap");
    else {
      state.pending = true;
      state.firstTapTime = t;
    }
  }
}
if (state.pending) ctx.requestNextFrame();
```

- Pulses: input `tap` fires on an upstream pulse or a false→true edge of a connected boolean (`ctx.pulsed`); taps on consecutive frames each count. Outputs `doubleTap` and `singleTap` last one frame, at most one pulse per output per frame.
- Timing: measured between tap frames (release to release) with `ctx.time`, so results match at 60 and 120 fps. Double Tap fires on the second tap's frame. Single Tap fires on the first frame where Interval has passed since a lone tap.
- Same-frame precedence: a tap that completes a double tap wins over the pending tap's expiry. If frames were dropped so a new tap arrives after the window closed, the old tap's Single Tap fires and the new tap starts its own wait on the same frame.
- Modes: when `tap` is connected, `layer` is ignored. Otherwise taps come from the pointer snapshot's tap rule (lifted on the layer after moving less than 10 pt). The two taps don't have to land near each other.
- Retargeting: Interval is read every frame, so changing it mid-wait moves the deadline.
- Frame 0: nothing is pending; a tap on frame 0 starts a wait.
- Edge cases: negative Interval counts as 0. With Interval 0, every tap pulses Single Tap on its own frame and Double Tap never fires. Two fingers lifting on the same frame count as one tap. A missing layer never taps.
- Loops: per-index state; a loop-replicated layer or a looped Tap gives loops of Double Tap and Single Tap.
- Disabled: no pulses; any waiting tap is discarded, so Single Tap doesn't fire later.
- Platform and simulation: same everywhere; simulation reads `pointer` InputEvents.
- Restart and dispose: the waiting tap clears; nothing to dispose.
- Coming from Origami: ports Tap and Delay map to `tap` and `interval`; the 0.3 s default is Origami's. Origami's pattern-file version fired both pulses when the window closed, and the built-in's exact timing is undocumented. Sonobe fires Double Tap immediately on the second tap.

State per loop index: `{ on: ctx.input("startOn"), blocked: false }`, created on frame 0, after a restart, and when a loop index first appears.

Each frame:
```
const snap = ctx.services.pointer(ctx.input("layer")); // null = whole screen
const enabled = ctx.input("enabled");
if (!enabled && (snap.down || snap.ended)) state.blocked = true;
const live = enabled && !state.blocked;
const before = state.on;
if (ctx.pulsed("turnOff")) state.on = false;
else if (ctx.pulsed("turnOn")) state.on = true;
else if ((live && snap.tapped) || ctx.pulsed("flip")) state.on = !state.on;
ctx.output("on", state.on);
ctx.output("down", live && snap.down);
if (state.on && !before) ctx.pulse("turnedOn");
if (!state.on && before) ctx.pulse("turnedOff");
if (!snap.down) state.blocked = false;
```

- Pulses: inputs `flip`, `turnOn`, `turnOff` fire on an upstream pulse or a false→true edge of a connected boolean (`ctx.pulsed`); pulses on consecutive frames each count. Outputs `turnedOn` and `turnedOff` fire only when the state actually changes, for one frame.
- Precedence (ARCHITECTURE.md §5.2, CONVENTIONS.md §18.5.2): turnOff > turnOn > (tap or flip). A tap and a Flip pulse on the same frame flip once, not twice.
- Tap rule: the same as Interaction's (began on the layer, moved less than 10 pt, not cancelled, lifted over the layer). Down is captured while the press is held.
- Start On is read only when state is created. Changing it later doesn't change the current state. Starting on doesn't pulse Turned On on frame 0.
- Frame 0: a tap or pulse on frame 0 applies on frame 0.
- Loops: per-index state, so a loop-replicated layer gives independent toggles (a checklist). New indices start at that index's Start On; removed indices drop their state.
- Disabled: taps are ignored and Down is false, but Flip, Turn On, and Turn Off still apply and On keeps its state. Decision: other patches can still reset a toggle that people can't tap. A press held during any disabled frame can't tap.
- Muted: On outputs Start On, Down false, no pulses, and state doesn't change.
- Edge cases: a missing layer never taps, but the pulse inputs still work.
- Platform and simulation: same everywhere; simulation reads `pointer` InputEvents.
- Restart and dispose: state resets to Start On; nothing to dispose.
- Coming from Origami: there's no single patch. It's Interaction's Tap wired into Switch's Flip, and importers keep those two patches.

State per loop index: `{ lastPosition: [0, 0], lastLocal: [0, 0] }`.

Each frame:
```
const layer = ctx.input("layer");
const snap = ctx.services.pointer(layer);
const hovering = ctx.input("enabled") && (layer === null ? mouseInsidePrototype() : snap.hovering);
if (hovering) {
  state.lastPosition = snap.position;
  state.lastLocal = snap.localPosition;
}
ctx.output("hovering", hovering);
ctx.output("position", state.lastPosition);
ctx.output("localPosition", state.lastLocal);
```

- Hover rule: a mouse pointer whose current hit chain contains the layer. Hovering pointers are re-hit-tested every frame against the previous frame's layout. The chain holds the front-most target plus its interactive ancestors, so children count, siblings in front block, and layers that are disabled, at opacity 0, or with Receives Touches off never hover.
- Pressed mouse: while a button is held, the pointer keeps hovering whatever it's over, like CSS `:hover`. The engine's `PointerSnapshot.hovering` must include pressed mouse pointers; today `PointerTracker` drops a pointer from hover tracking while it's pressed.
- Touch and pen pointers never hover, including after they lift. Telling them apart needs a `pointerType` on pointer InputEvents (contract request).
- Layer empty (`mouseInsidePrototype`): true while a mouse pointer's position is inside `[0, width] × [0, height]` of the root. The renderer reports a move just outside the prototype when the pointer leaves the viewer, which always clears hover (Origami v174 fixed stuck hover).
- Position: prototype coordinates. It updates only while hovering and holds after the pointer leaves; [0, 0] before the first hover. Local Position maps the same point through the layer's inverse world transform.
- Pulses: none. Wire Hovering into a Pulse patch for enter and leave events.
- Frame 0: false until the pointer first moves over the prototype.
- Loops: per copy of a loop-replicated layer, with per-index state.
- Edge cases: a missing layer never hovers.
- Disabled: Hovering false; positions hold.
- Muted: Hovering false and positions [0, 0]; Enabled is not passed through.
- Platform and simulation: desktop app and desktop browsers. Phones and tablets output false, which the docs cover. Simulation drives hover with `pointer` move events that have no press.
- Restart and dispose: state resets, and hover stays false until the pointer moves again. Nothing to dispose.
- Coming from Origami: ports Enable and Hover map to `enabled` and `hovering`. Origami's Position is relative to the parent's center.

Stateless: reads the engine's keyboard snapshot every frame.

Each frame:
```
const raw = ctx.input("key");
let parts;
if (raw === " ") parts = [" "];
else if (raw.trim() === "") parts = [];
else {
  parts = raw.split("+").map((p) => p.trim());
  if (parts.includes("")) parts = [...parts.filter((p) => p !== ""), "+"]; // "+" and "Shift++" watch the plus key
}
const names = parts.map(normalizeKey); // @sonobe/engine gestures/keyboard.ts
const pressed = ctx.services.keyboard().pressed;
ctx.output("down", ctx.input("enabled") && names.length > 0 && names.every((k) => pressed.has(k)));
```

- Normalization (`normalizeKey`): single characters lowercase and a space character is `Space`. Friendly aliases: `up`/`down`/`left`/`right` → arrows; `esc` → `Escape`; `return` → `Enter`; `delete` → `Backspace` as on Mac keyboards; `del`/`forwardDelete` → `Delete`; `cmd`/`command`/`meta` → `Meta`; `option`/`opt`/`alt` → `Alt`; `ctrl`/`control` → `Control`; `capslock`, `home`, `end`, `pageUp`, `pageDown`. `F1`–`F12` go uppercase. Other names pass through as DOM `KeyboardEvent.key` names (`ArrowUp`, `MediaPlayPause`). Parse once per distinct Key text; cache by string.
- Matching: Down is true while every named key is in `pressed`. Other held keys don't matter, so `a` stays down while Shift is also held. Keys are tracked by the character they produce (`event.key`), so with Shift held, digit keys arrive as symbols.
- Timing: Down turns true on the frame the last required key goes down and false on the frame any required key goes up. The renderer ignores auto-repeat, so a held key stays true without flicker.
- Pulses: none. Wiring Down into a pulse input fires once per press (a rising edge).
- Focus: the renderer delivers keys only while the viewer has focus. Keys typed into an editable Text Field aren't delivered, except navigation keys it passes through. When the window loses focus, every held key is released so nothing stays stuck.
- Frame 0: false. Keys already held when the prototype starts don't count until they're pressed again.
- Loops: a looped Key evaluates per index, for example `1` to `9` into a Loop Option Switch.
- Edge cases: empty Key → Down false, with no warning. Unknown names never match.
- Disabled: Down false.
- Muted: Down false; Enabled is not passed through.
- Platform and simulation: works anywhere with a hardware keyboard. Phones without one output false, which the docs cover. Browser and app shortcuts, like ⌘R to restart, may be taken before the prototype sees them. Simulation reads `key` InputEvents.
- Restart and dispose: the engine releases all keys on restart; nothing to dispose.
- Coming from Origami: ports Key and Down keep their names. Origami's tokens for special keys are undocumented, so the aliases accept the likely spellings (`up`, `shift`, `command`, `option`, `escape`, `delete`, `tab`, `capslock`). Combinations and Enabled are Sonobe additions.
